window.MLF_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Data Work","f":"p1-python-for-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After running all four cells once, you accidentally run data = [x * 2 for x in data] a second time. What does data now hold?","o":["The original values doubled once","The original values multiplied by four","A NameError, because data already exists","The original values, since cells are idempotent"],"a":1,"w":"The cell reads the current data from the kernel and doubles it again. Cells are not idempotent unless you write them that way."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Data Work","f":"p1-python-for-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the main reason to create a separate virtual environment for each project?","o":["It makes Python code run faster","It is required before Jupyter can start","It stops other people reading your code","Each project keeps its own package versions"],"a":3,"w":"Isolation is the point: upgrading pandas for one project cannot change another, and pip freeze records exactly what this project used."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Data Work","f":"p1-python-for-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A CSV file has a price column of whole numbers. What type is row[\"price\"] after reading it with csv.DictReader ?","o":["str","int","float","It depends on the first row"],"a":0,"w":"The csv module does no type conversion: every value is text, and an empty field is an empty string. Converting is your job, or pandas'."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Data Work","f":"p1-python-for-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Two separate scripts each run rng = np.random.default_rng(42) and then rng.random(5) . What do they print?","o":["Two different sets of five numbers","The same numbers, but only on the same machine","Exactly the same five numbers","Five copies of the number 0.42"],"a":2,"w":"A seeded generator is a deterministic sequence: the same seed and the same calls give the same numbers, on any machine running the same NumPy version."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Python for Data Work","f":"p1-python-for-data.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You are about to send a notebook to a colleague. Which single step best checks that its numbers are real?","o":["Clear all outputs so the file is smaller","Restart the kernel and run all cells in order","Run the last cell again to refresh the result","Save it twice to flush the kernel state"],"a":1,"w":"Only a clean top-to-bottom run proves the results do not depend on hidden state from cells run out of order, edited or deleted."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"NumPy Arrays & Vectorisation","f":"p2-numpy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the shape of A + B when A is (3, 4) and B is (4,) ?","o":["(4,)","(3,)","(3, 4)","It raises a ValueError"],"a":2,"w":"B is padded to (1, 4) and stretched down the three rows. This is the shape of subtracting column means from a feature matrix."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"NumPy Arrays & Vectorisation","f":"p2-numpy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"pred has shape (5, 1) and y has shape (5,) . What does pred - y produce?","o":["A (5, 5) matrix, with no error","A (5,) vector of errors","A (5, 1) column of errors","A ValueError about the shapes"],"a":0,"w":"Aligned from the right, (5, 1) and (1, 5) are both stretched to (5, 5). Every prediction is subtracted from every label, and nothing warns you."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"NumPy Arrays & Vectorisation","f":"p2-numpy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"X has shape (100, 8) . What is the shape of X.mean(axis=0) ?","o":["(100,)","(100, 8)","A single number","(8,)"],"a":3,"w":"The axis you name is the one collapsed. Averaging down the 100 rows leaves one mean per feature."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"NumPy Arrays & Vectorisation","f":"p2-numpy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After b = a[:, :2] and b[0, 0] = 99 , what happens to a[0, 0] ?","o":["It is unchanged, because b is a copy","It becomes 99, because b is a view of a","It becomes 99 only if a is a float array","The assignment raises an error"],"a":1,"w":"Basic slicing returns a view onto the same memory. A boolean mask or a list of indices would have returned a copy."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"NumPy Arrays & Vectorisation","f":"p2-numpy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the dtype of np.array([1, 2.5, 3]) ?","o":["int64, rounding 2.5 down to 2","object, since the values are mixed","float64, for every element","A different dtype for each element"],"a":2,"w":"An array has one dtype, chosen to hold every value. One float makes the whole array float64."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"pandas DataFrames","f":"p3-pandas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A freshly loaded DataFrame has the default index 0, 1, 2, … How many rows does df.loc[0:2] return?","o":["2","3","1","It depends on the dtypes"],"a":1,"w":"loc slices by label and includes the end label, so it returns rows 0, 1 and 2. iloc[0:2] would return two."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"pandas DataFrames","f":"p3-pandas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You left-join 5 orders to a customers table in which one customer ID appears twice, and that customer has 2 orders. How many rows come out?","o":["5","4","6","7"],"a":3,"w":"Each of that customer's 2 orders matches 2 customer rows, so 2 extra rows appear: 5 + 2 = 7, with no error."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"pandas DataFrames","f":"p3-pandas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Some rows have a missing city . What happens to them in df.groupby(\"city\")[\"amount\"].sum() ?","o":["They are left out of the result","They are added to the first city","They form a group named \"None\"","The groupby raises an error"],"a":0,"w":"By default groupby drops rows whose key is missing, so the totals shrink. dropna=False keeps them as a NaN group."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"pandas DataFrames","f":"p3-pandas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A CSV column holds whole-number prices, but one price is blank. What dtype does pandas give the column?","o":["int64, with the blank stored as 0","str, because of the blank","float64, with the blank as NaN","int64, with the row removed"],"a":2,"w":"pandas marks a missing number as NaN, which is a float, so the whole column becomes float64. It is often the first sign of missing data."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"pandas DataFrames","f":"p3-pandas.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does validate=\"many_to_one\" do in orders.merge(customers, on=\"customer_id\", validate=\"many_to_one\") ?","o":["It removes duplicate customers before joining","It raises an error if customer keys repeat","It keeps only the first match for each order","It checks that every order found a customer"],"a":1,"w":"It is an assertion, not a fix: if the right-hand keys are not unique, pandas raises MergeError instead of multiplying rows."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Linear Algebra for ML","f":"p4-linear-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the dot product of [1, 2, 3] and [4, 5, 6] ?","o":["[4, 10, 18]","21","32","90"],"a":2,"w":"1×4 + 2×5 + 3×6 = 4 + 10 + 18 = 32. The list [4, 10, 18] is the elementwise product, before summing."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Linear Algebra for ML","f":"p4-linear-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"X has shape (100, 8) and w has shape (8,) . What is the shape of X @ w ?","o":["(100,)","(8,)","(100, 8)","It raises a shape error"],"a":0,"w":"The inner sizes (8 and 8) match and disappear, leaving one value per row: one prediction for each of the 100 examples."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Linear Algebra for ML","f":"p4-linear-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Two non-zero vectors are perpendicular. What is their cosine similarity?","o":["1","−1","0.5","0"],"a":3,"w":"The angle is 90°, and cos 90° = 0. Their dot product is 0 too, whatever their lengths."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Linear Algebra for ML","f":"p4-linear-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the L1 norm of the vector [3, −4] ?","o":["5","7","−1","25"],"a":1,"w":"L1 adds absolute values: |3| + |−4| = 7. The L2 norm would be √(9 + 16) = 5."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Linear Algebra for ML","f":"p4-linear-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A dataset has a price-in-pounds column and an identical price-in-pence column (×100). What happens to least squares?","o":["It fails to make any predictions","It doubles the weight of price automatically","Many weight vectors give the same fit","Nothing: the duplicate column is ignored"],"a":2,"w":"The columns are linearly dependent, so XᵀX is singular. The fitted predictions are still well defined, but weight can be traded freely between the two columns, so the weights are not unique."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Derivatives & Gradients","f":"p5-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the derivative of L(w) = (w − 2)² at w = 5 ?","o":["9","3","10","6"],"a":3,"w":"By the chain rule the derivative is 2(w − 2) × 1, which at w = 5 is 2 × 3 = 6. The loss itself is 9."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Derivatives & Gradients","f":"p5-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the gradient of f(x, y) = x² + 3y² at the point (1, 1)?","o":["(1, 3)","(2, 6)","(2, 3)","8"],"a":1,"w":"∂f/∂x = 2x = 2 and ∂f/∂y = 6y = 6. A gradient is a vector with one entry per variable, not a single number."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Derivatives & Gradients","f":"p5-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"To make a loss smaller, in which direction should a small step change the parameters?","o":["Opposite to the gradient","Along the gradient","Perpendicular to the gradient","Along the largest single parameter"],"a":0,"w":"The gradient points uphill, so a small step against it is the fastest local way down. A step perpendicular to it barely changes the loss."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Derivatives & Gradients","f":"p5-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"For one example with feature x and label y , what is the derivative of (w·x − y)² with respect to w ?","o":["2(w·x − y)","2w·x","2(w·x − y) · x","(w·x − y) · x²"],"a":2,"w":"Outer derivative 2(w·x − y), times the inner derivative of w·x − y with respect to w, which is x. Forgetting the inner factor is the classic chain-rule bug."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Derivatives & Gradients","f":"p5-calculus.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You check a gradient with a central difference in float64. Why is h = 10⁻¹³ a worse choice than h = 10⁻⁵?","o":["The formula stops being valid below 10⁻¹⁰","Rounding noise is divided by a tiny h","It is slower to compute with a small h","It is not worse; smaller h is always better"],"a":1,"w":"f(w + h) and f(w − h) then agree in almost every digit, so their difference is mostly rounding error, and dividing by 2h magnifies it."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability","f":"p6-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"1% of cases are positive. A detector catches 99% of positives and flags 5% of negatives. Roughly what share of flagged cases are positive?","o":["99%","95%","17%","1%"],"a":2,"w":"Per 10,000: 99 true flags and 495 false ones, so 99 / 594 ≈ 16.7%. The large negative pool supplies most of the flags."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability","f":"p6-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A and B are independent, with P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 0.5. What is P(A and B)?","o":["0.15","0.8","0.3","It cannot be known"],"a":0,"w":"For independent events the joint probability is the product, 0.3 × 0.5 = 0.15. Without independence you would need more information."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability","f":"p6-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which distribution best describes the number of support tickets opened in an hour?","o":["Normal","Bernoulli","Uniform","Poisson"],"a":3,"w":"A count of independent events in a fixed interval, with some average rate, is the classic Poisson case."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability","f":"p6-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the expected value of one roll of a fair six-sided die?","o":["3","3.5","6","2.92"],"a":1,"w":"The average of 1 to 6 is 21 / 6 = 3.5, even though no single roll can show it. 2.92 is the variance."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Probability","f":"p6-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"X and Y are independent, each with variance 4. What is the variance of X + Y?","o":["4","16","8","2√2"],"a":2,"w":"Variances of independent variables add: 4 + 4 = 8. The standard deviation is √8 ≈ 2.83, not 2 + 2."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Statistics for ML","f":"p7-statistics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which statement about a correctly computed 95% confidence interval is right?","o":["95% of the data falls inside the interval","The method catches the true value 95% of the time","The true value moves around inside it 95% of the time","A new sample's mean will fall inside it with 95% probability"],"a":1,"w":"The 95% describes the procedure over repeated samples. It is not about the spread of the data, and the true value is fixed, not random."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Statistics for ML","f":"p7-statistics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You want the confidence interval for a mean to be half as wide. Roughly how much more data do you need?","o":["Twice as much","√2 times as much","Eight times as much","Four times as much"],"a":3,"w":"Width is proportional to 1/√n, so halving it needs √n to double, which means n quadruples."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Statistics for ML","f":"p7-statistics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"House prices in a town are strongly right-skewed. Which single number best describes a typical house?","o":["The median price","The mean price","The standard deviation of price","The maximum price"],"a":0,"w":"A few expensive houses pull the mean up; the median stays with the middle of the market."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Statistics for ML","f":"p7-statistics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Machines with more memory show more crashes. Which explanation is a confounder?","o":["Memory chips cause software to crash","Crashing machines are upgraded with more memory","Heavy workloads get big machines and also crash more","The correlation is too weak to mean anything"],"a":2,"w":"A confounder drives both variables. The second option is reverse causation, a different trap."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Statistics for ML","f":"p7-statistics.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In a bootstrap of a test set's accuracy, what does each resample consist of?","o":["A random half of the rows, without replacement","n rows drawn with replacement, kept whole","The predictions shuffled against the labels","New data collected from the population"],"a":1,"w":"Each bootstrap sample has the original size, drawn with replacement, and keeps each prediction with its own label."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Exploratory Data Analysis","f":"p8-eda-plotting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A price column has a mean of £789k and a median of £230k. What does that suggest?","o":["A long right tail of expensive houses","Most houses cost about £789k","The column contains negative prices","The data is symmetric around £500k"],"a":0,"w":"A mean far above the median means a few large values are pulling it up: a right-skewed column. A log-scale histogram is the next view."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Exploratory Data Analysis","f":"p8-eda-plotting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A floor-area histogram shows a spike of values at exactly 0. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["Some properties are plots of land","The values were rounded down","0 was used as a placeholder for unknown","They are outliers that help the model"],"a":2,"w":"A house cannot have zero floor area. A spike at a round impossible value is almost always a placeholder, and should become a missing value."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Exploratory Data Analysis","f":"p8-eda-plotting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Most areas fall between 50 and 200 m², but a second cluster sits around 600 to 2,000. What is the likely cause?","o":["A separate market of mansions","A data-entry typo in a few rows","Normal skew in house sizes","Some areas were recorded in square feet"],"a":3,"w":"One square metre is about 10.76 square feet, so a hump at roughly ten times the main one is the signature of mixed units."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Exploratory Data Analysis","f":"p8-eda-plotting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In a pandas or matplotlib box plot, how far do the whiskers reach by default?","o":["To the minimum and maximum","To the furthest points within 1.5 × IQR of the box","Two standard deviations from the mean","To the 5th and 95th percentiles"],"a":1,"w":"The default is 1.5 times the interquartile range beyond each quartile; points further out are drawn individually."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Exploratory Data Analysis","f":"p8-eda-plotting.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A stamp-duty column predicts sale price almost perfectly. Why should it not be used to predict prices of houses not yet sold?","o":["It has too many missing values","It is measured in different units","It is calculated from the sale price itself","It is not correlated with price"],"a":2,"w":"Stamp duty is computed from the agreed price, so it does not exist until the price does. Using it is target leakage."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Machine Learning Is","f":"01-what-is-ml.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Predicting how many days a parcel will take to arrive is which kind of task?","o":["Classification","Regression","Clustering","Dimensionality reduction"],"a":1,"w":"The label is a number, so it is supervised regression. Predicting \"late or on time\" instead would make it classification."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Machine Learning Is","f":"01-what-is-ml.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A shop wants to group customers by purchase behaviour, with no predefined groups. What kind of learning is that?","o":["Supervised classification","Supervised regression","Reinforcement learning","Unsupervised clustering"],"a":3,"w":"There are no labels to learn from, only features. Finding groups of similar examples is clustering."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Machine Learning Is","f":"01-what-is-ml.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which problem is least suited to machine learning?","o":["Calculating income tax from published bands","Flagging fraudulent card transactions","Estimating a house's sale price","Spotting spam in email"],"a":0,"w":"Tax bands are an exact, published rule. Writing the rule is cheaper, exact and auditable; learning it from examples could only add errors."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Machine Learning Is","f":"01-what-is-ml.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does it mean for a model to generalise well?","o":["It scores well on the data it was trained on","It uses as many features as possible","It performs well on new data it has not seen","It works for every kind of problem"],"a":2,"w":"Training performance can come from memorising. Generalisation is measured on held-out examples from the same source."},{"n":1,"ti":"What Machine Learning Is","f":"01-what-is-ml.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A bot detector trained on last month's traffic misses many bots this month, after bots changed behaviour. What is the most direct fix?","o":["Lower the threshold until every bot is caught","Retrain on recent, labelled traffic","Add more of last month's data to training","Switch to a more complex model family"],"a":1,"w":"The model learned last month's patterns. More old data teaches the same lesson; recent labelled examples teach the new one."},{"n":2,"ti":"The ML Workflow End to End","f":"02-ml-workflow.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why measure a baseline such as \"always predict the median\" before training a model?","o":["scikit-learn requires it before fitting","It improves the model's final accuracy","It shows whether a model adds anything","It replaces the need for a test set"],"a":2,"w":"A model's score only means something next to what a trivial predictor achieves. An MAE of $50k is good against $88.6k and poor against $55k."},{"n":2,"ti":"The ML Workflow End to End","f":"02-ml-workflow.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In this project's experiment log, which single change reduced the error the most?","o":["Switching from linear regression to gradient boosting","Tuning the gradient-boosting hyperparameters","Adding three engineered features to boosting","Predicting log(value) with gradient boosting"],"a":0,"w":"Linear to boosting took the error from about $53k to $32k. Tuning added about $2k; engineered features and the log target changed boosting very little."},{"n":2,"ti":"The ML Workflow End to End","f":"02-ml-workflow.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When should the test set be used in this workflow?","o":["After each experiment, to track progress","To choose between the last two candidate models","At the start, to check the data is sensible","Once, after every decision has been made"],"a":3,"w":"Any decision informed by the test score makes it part of model selection, and the score stops being an honest estimate of performance on new data."},{"n":2,"ti":"The ML Workflow End to End","f":"02-ml-workflow.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"One configuration scores an MAE of $70.1k ± $39.2k across five folds. What should you conclude?","o":["It is about $70k worse than the baseline","At least one fold went badly wrong; investigate","The standard deviation can be ignored","It is the best model, as its spread is widest"],"a":1,"w":"Other configurations vary by about $1k between folds. A spread of $39k means one fold produced wild predictions, here from extreme rows on a log scale."},{"n":2,"ti":"The ML Workflow End to End","f":"02-ml-workflow.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The best model's error is $15.8k for cheap districts and $70.5k for districts at the $500k cap. Which workflow step found this?","o":["The baseline","Cross-validation","Error analysis by segment","The train/test split"],"a":2,"w":"An overall MAE of $29k hides how the error is distributed. Slicing it by price band shows where the model fails and why."},{"n":3,"ti":"Train, Validation & Test Splits","f":"03-data-splits.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the validation set for?","o":["Fitting the model's parameters","Choosing between models and settings","Reporting the final performance estimate","Storing rows with missing labels"],"a":1,"w":"The training set fits parameters, the validation set guides choices, and the test set gives the final, untouched estimate."},{"n":3,"ti":"Train, Validation & Test Splits","f":"03-data-splits.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A dataset has ten X-ray images per patient, and the model will be used on new patients. How should it be split?","o":["Randomly by image","Stratified by diagnosis only","By the date each image was taken","By patient, so no patient is on both sides"],"a":3,"w":"Images of the same patient share anatomy and equipment quirks. Splitting by patient makes the test set look like the new patients the model will meet."},{"n":3,"ti":"Train, Validation & Test Splits","f":"03-data-splits.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A random split of daily sales gives an error of 9.6; a time-based split gives 23.2. Which better predicts the error of a forecast for the next few months?","o":["23.2, from the time-based split","9.6, from the random split","Their average, about 16","Neither: only the training error matters"],"a":0,"w":"A forecast has no future data to lean on. The time-based split reproduces that; in the simulator the true error on the next 146 days was 23.0."},{"n":3,"ti":"Train, Validation & Test Splits","f":"03-data-splits.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does stratify=y guarantee in train_test_split ?","o":["That the model will reach the same recall on both sets","That no customer appears on both sides","That each class has the same share in both parts","That later rows end up in the test set"],"a":2,"w":"Stratification controls class proportions. It does nothing about groups or time, and it cannot make a metric on a handful of positives precise."},{"n":3,"ti":"Train, Validation & Test Splits","f":"03-data-splits.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why fit a scaler on the training rows only, rather than on the whole dataset before splitting?","o":["Because scalers fail on small datasets","So the test rows cannot shape training","So that training runs faster","It makes no difference to the evaluation"],"a":1,"w":"Statistics computed with the test rows, such as their mean and spread, carry information about them into training: a small but real leak that pipelines prevent."},{"n":4,"ti":"Linear Regression","f":"04-linear-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does ordinary least squares minimise?","o":["The sum of squared residuals","The sum of absolute residuals","The largest single residual","The number of wrong predictions"],"a":0,"w":"It squares each residual and adds them up. Absolute residuals give least absolute deviations, a different and more outlier-resistant fit."},{"n":4,"ti":"Linear Regression","f":"04-linear-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model has a coefficient of 14.5 (in £k) for bedrooms. What does it mean?","o":["Adding a bedroom to a house raises its value by £14.5k","Bedrooms are the most important feature","With other features fixed, the prediction rises £14.5k per bedroom","A house with no bedrooms is worth £14.5k"],"a":2,"w":"A coefficient describes the model's prediction with the other features held fixed. It is not a causal effect, and its size cannot be compared with features in other units."},{"n":4,"ti":"Linear Regression","f":"04-linear-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A residual plot shows residuals that are positive at both ends and negative in the middle. What does it suggest?","o":["The model is overfitting","There are too many features","The errors are perfectly random","The relationship is curved, not straight"],"a":3,"w":"A U-shaped pattern means a straight line under-predicts at the extremes and over-predicts in the middle: the true relationship bends."},{"n":4,"ti":"Linear Regression","f":"04-linear-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Floor area is included both in m² and in ft². What happens to the two coefficients?","o":["Both become exactly half their usual value","They can become large and opposite, and unstable","One is set to zero automatically","Predictions become badly wrong"],"a":1,"w":"The columns are nearly collinear, so weight can move between them with almost no change in fit. Predictions stay fine; individual coefficients become meaningless."},{"n":4,"ti":"Linear Regression","f":"04-linear-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"One record in a dataset of 40 has a price ten times too low. Why does it affect least squares so much?","o":["Least squares ignores small residuals entirely","The intercept is fixed by the first record","Its residual is squared, so it dominates the total","It does not: least squares is robust to outliers"],"a":2,"w":"A residual four times larger costs sixteen times more. Moving the line towards that one point reduces the total more than it costs on all the others."},{"n":5,"ti":"Gradient Descent","f":"05-gradient-descent.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the gradient-descent update for weights w , learning rate η and loss L?","o":["w ← w + η · ∇L(w)","w ← w − η · ∇L(w)","w ← ∇L(w) / η","w ← w − η · L(w)"],"a":1,"w":"The gradient points uphill, so the step goes against it, scaled by the learning rate. The loss value itself is not used in the step."},{"n":5,"ti":"Gradient Descent","f":"05-gradient-descent.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"After a few steps the training loss is growing every step and soon becomes inf . What is the most likely cause?","o":["The learning rate is too small","There are too few training rows","The model has reached its minimum","The learning rate is too large"],"a":3,"w":"Each step overshoots the minimum by more than it gains, so the loss grows geometrically: divergence. Reduce the learning rate, and check feature scaling."},{"n":5,"ti":"Gradient Descent","f":"05-gradient-descent.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"One feature ranges from 0 to 1 and another from 0 to 10,000. Why does gradient descent struggle?","o":["A rate safe for the steep direction crawls along the shallow one","Large numbers make the gradient impossible to compute","The loss has no minimum when features differ in scale","The small feature is ignored entirely by the gradient"],"a":0,"w":"Different scales stretch the loss surface into a narrow valley. The learning rate is capped by the steepest direction, so progress along the shallow one is tiny."},{"n":5,"ti":"Gradient Descent","f":"05-gradient-descent.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Compared with batch gradient descent, what does stochastic gradient descent (one row per step) trade?","o":["More memory for a more exact gradient","Fewer steps per epoch for a smoother path","Cheap, noisy steps for exact, expensive ones","A guaranteed minimum for faster convergence"],"a":2,"w":"Each SGD step uses one row, so it is fast but its gradient is a noisy estimate; the path wanders and needs a decaying learning rate to settle."},{"n":5,"ti":"Gradient Descent","f":"05-gradient-descent.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A dataset has 10,000 rows and you use mini-batches of 100. How many steps make one epoch?","o":["1","100","10,000","1,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"An epoch is one full pass over the data: 10,000 / 100 = 100 steps."},{"n":6,"ti":"Loss Functions","f":"06-loss-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model with no features must predict one constant. Which constant minimises mean squared error?","o":["The median of the targets","The most common target value","The mean of the targets","The midpoint of the smallest and largest"],"a":2,"w":"The mean minimises the sum of squared deviations. The median minimises the sum of absolute deviations."},{"n":6,"ti":"Loss Functions","f":"06-loss-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You add one extreme outlier to the training data. Which loss's best constant prediction moves the most?","o":["Mean squared error","Mean absolute error","Huber loss with a small δ","Quantile loss at τ = 0.5"],"a":0,"w":"Squared error's minimum is the mean, which moves in proportion to the outlier's size. The other three move by at most one position in the sorted data."},{"n":6,"ti":"Loss Functions","f":"06-loss-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Running out of stock costs four times as much as one unit of excess. Which quantile of demand should the model predict?","o":["The 25th percentile","The median","The mean","The 80th percentile"],"a":3,"w":"τ = cost of under-prediction / total = 4 / (4 + 1) = 0.8: stock enough to cover demand 80% of the time."},{"n":6,"ti":"Loss Functions","f":"06-loss-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A classifier gives probability 0.01 to an event that then happens. What is its log loss for that example?","o":["0.01","About 4.6","0.99","Exactly 1"],"a":1,"w":"Log loss is −log(p) for the true class: −ln(0.01) ≈ 4.6. A confident mistake costs far more than a hesitant one."},{"n":6,"ti":"Loss Functions","f":"06-loss-functions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the Huber loss do?","o":["It ignores every error smaller than δ","It squares every error, then takes a square root","Squared for small errors, linear for large ones","It counts only whether the error is positive"],"a":2,"w":"Inside ±δ it behaves like squared error, smooth near zero; beyond δ it grows linearly, so outliers have limited pull."},{"n":7,"ti":"Logistic Regression","f":"07-logistic-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"The linear score for a customer is z = 0. What probability does logistic regression give?","o":["0","0.5","1","It depends on the intercept"],"a":1,"w":"σ(0) = 1 / (1 + e⁰) = 0.5. The decision boundary at a 0.5 threshold is exactly where the score is zero."},{"n":7,"ti":"Logistic Regression","f":"07-logistic-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A coefficient for \"support tickets\" is 0.5. What does exp(0.5) ≈ 1.65 mean?","o":["Each ticket adds 1.65 percentage points to churn probability","Churn is 1.65 times as likely for every customer","Each ticket adds 50% to the probability of churn","Each ticket multiplies the odds of churn by about 1.65"],"a":3,"w":"Coefficients act on the log-odds, so their exponentials multiply the odds. The change in probability depends on the starting probability."},{"n":7,"ti":"Logistic Regression","f":"07-logistic-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is logistic regression fitted iteratively rather than with a formula?","o":["Setting the log-loss gradient to zero has no closed-form solution","Its loss has many local minima that must be searched","Probabilities cannot be represented as matrices","scikit-learn does not implement the normal equation"],"a":0,"w":"The equations are non-linear in the weights, so they are solved numerically. The loss is convex, so there are no false minima to worry about."},{"n":7,"ti":"Logistic Regression","f":"07-logistic-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model reaches 91.6% accuracy on churn data where 9.1% of customers churn. What should you conclude?","o":["The model is excellent and ready to deploy","The model is overfitting badly","It barely beats predicting \"no churn\" for all","Accuracy should be multiplied by the churn rate"],"a":2,"w":"Always predicting \"no\" scores 90.9%. Accuracy hides how well the rare class is caught; precision, recall and the model's probabilities tell you that."},{"n":7,"ti":"Logistic Regression","f":"07-logistic-regression.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On training data where the classes are perfectly separable, what happens to unregularised logistic regression's weights?","o":["They shrink towards zero","They grow without limit as the loss keeps falling","They settle at a unique finite value","They become negative"],"a":1,"w":"A steeper sigmoid always lowers the loss when no example is misclassified, so the optimum is at infinity. An L2 penalty makes it finite."},{"n":8,"ti":"Classification Metrics","f":"08-classification-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model flags 200 transactions; 50 are fraud. There were 80 frauds in total. What are precision and recall?","o":["Precision 0.25, recall 0.625","Precision 0.625, recall 0.25","Precision 0.40, recall 0.25","Precision 0.25, recall 0.40"],"a":0,"w":"Precision = 50 / 200 = 0.25 of the flags are real. Recall = 50 / 80 = 0.625 of the frauds were caught."},{"n":8,"ti":"Classification Metrics","f":"08-classification-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"You lower the decision threshold from 0.5 to 0.3. What usually happens?","o":["Precision and recall both rise","Precision rises and recall falls","Recall rises and precision falls","Neither changes; only accuracy does"],"a":2,"w":"More examples are flagged, so more positives are caught (recall up) and more negatives are swept in (precision usually down)."},{"n":8,"ti":"Classification Metrics","f":"08-classification-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does a ROC-AUC of 0.5 mean?","o":["Half of the predictions are correct","The model is perfect at a threshold of 0.5","Precision equals recall","The model ranks no better than chance"],"a":3,"w":"AUC is the probability a random positive scores above a random negative; 0.5 is a coin toss."},{"n":8,"ti":"Classification Metrics","f":"08-classification-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Fraud becomes ten times rarer, but the model's scores for each class stay the same. What happens to the curves?","o":["Both the ROC and the PR curves get worse","ROC stays the same; the PR curve drops","ROC drops; the PR curve stays the same","Neither changes"],"a":1,"w":"ROC uses rates within each class, which do not change. Precision depends on how many negatives there are to produce false alarms, so the PR curve falls."},{"n":8,"ti":"Classification Metrics","f":"08-classification-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Analysts can investigate 300 alerts a day. Which way of choosing the threshold fits best?","o":["Use 0.5, the default","Maximise accuracy on the test set","Flag the 300 highest scores each day","Choose the threshold with the highest recall"],"a":2,"w":"A capacity limit fixes the volume. Flagging the top 300 uses the model's ranking where it matters most, and the threshold is chosen without the test set."},{"n":9,"ti":"Regression Metrics & Baselines","f":"09-regression-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When is RMSE equal to MAE?","o":["When the model is unbiased","When every error has the same size","When there are no outliers in the features","Never: RMSE is always larger"],"a":1,"w":"RMSE ≥ MAE always, with equality only when all absolute errors are equal. The more uneven the errors, the bigger the gap."},{"n":9,"ti":"Regression Metrics & Baselines","f":"09-regression-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is MAPE a poor metric for products that often sell 0 or 1 unit?","o":["It ignores small products entirely","It is measured in units, not percentages","It cannot be computed in scikit-learn","Dividing by tiny actuals inflates or breaks it"],"a":3,"w":"An error of 2 units on an item that sold 1 is a 200% error, and an actual of 0 makes it undefined. Those items dominate the average."},{"n":9,"ti":"Regression Metrics & Baselines","f":"09-regression-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model's R² on the test set is 0. What does that mean?","o":["It is no better than predicting the test mean","Every prediction is exactly right","Its errors are all zero on average","It explains half of the variance"],"a":0,"w":"R² compares the model's squared error with that of the constant mean. 0 means equal; a negative R² means worse than that constant."},{"n":9,"ti":"Regression Metrics & Baselines","f":"09-regression-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which baseline should a daily sales forecast with a strong weekly pattern beat?","o":["The overall average of all past days","Yesterday's sales","Sales on the same weekday last week","Zero sales every day"],"a":2,"w":"The seasonal naive forecast captures the weekly pattern for free and is a demanding baseline. A model that cannot beat it adds nothing."},{"n":9,"ti":"Regression Metrics & Baselines","f":"09-regression-metrics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Model A has the lower MAE; model B has the lower RMSE. What does that tell you?","o":["One of the metrics has been computed wrongly","A is usually closer, but has some larger misses than B","B is better in every respect","The two models make identical errors"],"a":1,"w":"Lower MAE means smaller typical errors; lower RMSE means fewer or smaller big errors. A's error distribution has a heavier tail."}];
